{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And He said.</b> Now He mentions the beginning of the commandments that were through Moses and Aaron, for they alone were the prophets of the Torah, for after them no commandment was renewed, except if it was for a temporary need, like the matter of Gideon and Elijah. And let it not arise in your heart, the matter of the song that was in the House of HaShem by the hand of David, for it was like a arrangement, and a foundation that David and Solomon established, and thus it is written concerning their faithfulness and not their prophecy. And the Gaon [Saadia] said that they had a tradition in their hands from the mouth of Moses, that a king may arise and introduce musical instruments, and like it: \"you shall not carry a burden out of your houses on the day of the Sabbath\" (Jeremiah 17:22)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The Hebrew באמונתם (\"their faithfulness/trust\") is unusual in this context; read hyper-literally as written. Ibn Ezra invokes Jeremiah 17:22 as a case where a king's enactment (here Hezekiah's) carries authority grounded in Mosaic tradition."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:33:26.690085452Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}